0215 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360215 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186359506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360215 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "83e90b7a-7c25-529e-9722-30e5c26dc7c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360771 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360771 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97215" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360771 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360771 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨6a+4b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=2+4\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨c,b⟩=-5-3\\text{i}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360771 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "589fd79e-dcf6-5066-9979-9da6a025b573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360771 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6a+4b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨6a,c⟩+⟨4b,c⟩ \\\\  & =6⟨a,c⟩+4⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6⟨a,c⟩+4⟨b,c⟩ & =6\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+4\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨c,b⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+4\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩} & =6(\\overset{―}{2+4\\text{i}})+4\\overset{―}{(-5-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =6(2-4\\text{i})+4(-5+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =12-24\\text{i}-20+12\\text{i} \\\\  & =-8-12\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360775 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360775 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360775 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360775 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360775 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d89a9f6-d33c-56fa-8ee8-b25b137af49f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360775 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $a=0$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360784 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360784 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97303" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360784 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360784 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨b,a⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨-ia,b⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360784 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8247f7d2-00cd-54b9-9bd1-43c13383de3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360784 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. A complex  inner product is linear  in the first argument,  so we have \n\n$$\n⟨-ia,b⟩=-i⟨a,b⟩\\ne i⟨a,b⟩,\n$$\n\nin general.\n\n- Statement III is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
